 ## Backup confidentiality and recovery usability
 
-On November 11, Trust Wallet responded to worse_query, saying it encrypts recovery phrases and private keys on the device and considers its iOS users unaffected by this flaw. For a container-file read, there is a difference between obtaining ciphertext and obtaining the decrypted secrets. Once the device is compromised, plaintext in use or credentials entered by the user may be exposed. Trust Wallet's optional encrypted cloud backup uses a password chosen by the user. Recovery should still take place on a trusted device. [Coverage of Trust Wallet's statement](https://www.tokenpost.com/news/technology/53954) · [Trust Wallet on encryption](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/cryptography)
+On November 11, Trust Wallet responded to worse_query, saying it encrypts recovery phrases and private keys on the device and considers its iOS users unaffected by this flaw. A file read alone yields ciphertext while its keys remain protected. Once the device is compromised, plaintext in use or credentials entered by the user may be exposed. Trust Wallet's optional encrypted cloud backup uses a password chosen by the user. Recovery should still take place on a trusted device. [Coverage of Trust Wallet's statement](https://www.tokenpost.com/news/technology/53954) · [Trust Wallet on encryption](https://trustwallet.com/glossary/cryptography)
 
 Apple sets the boundaries for the Secure Enclave, iCloud Keychain, and Data Protection. A wallet can choose the right OS protections and key storage and can add its own encryption. The trustworthiness of the device still depends on system security. As Apple offers passkey sync, the Passwords app, and account recovery, some protections become common platform features. [Apple on Keychain data protection](https://support.apple.com/en-is/guide/security/secb0694df1a/web) · [Apple on passkey security](https://support.apple.com/en-ie/102195)
 
-There is work to do on recovery. In an interview, Trust Wallet's CEO raised jargon first, then cited confidence in recovery as a barrier to self-custody adoption. Most users have a hard time telling whether their backup will restore. Backup health checks, periodic drills, and **recovery on a new phone in 90 seconds** give the product something to demonstrate and measure. The wallet can build them into account recovery. [Interview with Trust Wallet's CEO](https://cryptopotato.com/self-custody-has-won-the-argument-now-it-has-to-work-trust-wallet-ceo-interview/)
+Recovery is an opportunity for Trust Wallet to improve the product. In an interview, Trust Wallet's CEO raised jargon first, then cited confidence in recovery as a barrier to self-custody adoption. Most users have a hard time telling whether their backup will restore. Backup health checks, periodic drills, and **recovery on a new phone in 90 seconds** give Trust Wallet concrete ways to demonstrate how recovery works and measure its performance. [Interview with Trust Wallet's CEO](https://cryptopotato.com/self-custody-has-won-the-argument-now-it-has-to-work-trust-wallet-ceo-interview/)
 
 ## Account features Trust Wallet already has
 
-Trust Wallet began rolling out the SWIFT beta on **February 19, 2024**. It uses an ERC-4337 smart account and replaces the manually managed 12-word recovery phrase with a passkey. The documented device migration requires iCloud Keychain or Google Password Manager and access to the same cloud account. Trust Wallet also open-sourced Barz, its smart account implementation. Its public materials still present the seed phrase-controlled externally owned account (EOA) as the standard path and list SWIFT as an option. This comparison relies on documented onboarding flows. [SWIFT launch announcement](https://trustwallet.com/ru/blog/company/predstavlyaem-trust-wallet-swift) · [SWIFT recovery documentation](https://trustwallet.com/blog/company/trust-wallet-swift-understanding-passkeys-and-recoverability) · [Barz and account abstraction](https://trustwallet.com/blog/developers/2025-account-abstraction-trends)
